## Why Use Your Phone as an Android TV Remote Over Wi-Fi?

Losing a physical remote is frustrating — and replacing one from a TV manufacturer can cost $20–$50. But there's a better solution hiding in your pocket. Your Android phone can become a fully functional remote control for Android TV and Google TV devices using your home Wi-Fi network. No Bluetooth pairing headaches, no line-of-sight requirements, and no extra hardware needed.

Wi-Fi-based remote control has several advantages over traditional infrared or Bluetooth remotes. The control signals travel over your local network, meaning the TV doesn't even need to be in the same room. You get a full keyboard for searching, touchpad input, D-pad navigation, and media controls — all from an app on your phone.

This guide walks you through the entire connection process, step by step.

---

## What You'll Need Before You Start

Before diving into the setup, make sure you have the following:

- An Android smartphone running Android 5.0 or later
- An Android TV or Google TV device (Chromecast with Google TV, Sony Bravia, Hisense, NVIDIA SHIELD, Xiaomi Mi Box, etc.)
- Both devices connected to the **same Wi-Fi network** — this is the most critical requirement
- The TV powered on and showing the home screen or any active interface

---

## Step 1: Download the App from the Play Store

The first step is to install **TV Remote for Android TV** on your phone. You can find it on the Google Play Store:

👉 [Download TV Remote for Android TV](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.hari.androidtvremote)

The app is free to download and requires no account sign-up. Once installed, open it — you'll land on the device discovery screen.

---

## Step 2: Ensure Both Devices Are on the Same Wi-Fi Network

This step is the single most common reason the connection fails. Your phone and your TV **must be on the exact same Wi-Fi network**.

To verify on Android TV:
1. Press the Home button on your physical remote (if available)
2. Go to **Settings → Network & Internet**
3. Note the network name (SSID) shown

On your phone:
1. Open **Settings → Wi-Fi**
2. Confirm you're connected to the same SSID

> **Important:** If your router broadcasts both 2.4GHz and 5GHz bands with different names, make sure both devices are on the same band, or use the same unified SSID if your router supports band steering.

---

## Step 3: Open the App and Auto-Discover Your TV

Once both devices share the same network, open **TV Remote for Android TV** on your phone. The app uses Android's built-in **Remote Device Protocol** (the same protocol Google's own remote uses) to broadcast a discovery request on your local network.

Within a few seconds, your TV should appear in the device list. The app displays:
- The TV's device name (e.g., "Living Room TV" or the model name)
- The TV's local IP address
- The TV's type (Android TV or Google TV)

Tap your TV from the list to initiate pairing.

---

## Step 4: Confirm the PIN on Your TV Screen

When you select your TV in the app, a **4-digit PIN code** will appear on your television screen. This is a one-time security measure to ensure only authorized devices can control your TV.

Enter that PIN in the app on your phone and tap **Confirm**. The pairing is saved — you won't need to re-enter it the next time you connect. The app remembers paired TVs and reconnects automatically.

---

## Step 5: Start Using Your Phone as a Remote

You're now connected! The app's main screen provides:

- **D-pad navigation** — large, tap-friendly directional controls
- **Media controls** — play, pause, rewind, fast-forward
- **Volume controls** — up, down, and mute
- **Home, Back, and Recents** buttons
- **Full keyboard** — tap the keyboard icon to type on your TV instantly
- **Touchpad mode** — swipe to navigate, tap to select

You can switch between D-pad and touchpad mode at any time.

