## Troubleshooting: What to Do When the TV Isn't Found

If your TV doesn't appear in the device list after 10–15 seconds, try these fixes:

### TV Not Found — Checklist

| Problem | Solution |
|---|---|
| Phone and TV on different networks | Connect both to the same Wi-Fi SSID |
| TV is off or in standby mode | Power on the TV fully, go to the home screen |
| Android TV Remote Service disabled | Enable it in TV Settings → Apps |
| Firewall or router isolation | Disable AP/client isolation in router settings |
| VPN active on phone | Disable VPN — it routes traffic off local network |
| App needs refresh | Close and reopen the app, or tap "Refresh" |

### Enabling Android TV Remote Service

On some devices, the **Android TV Remote Service** may be disabled by default or may have been turned off by a system update. To enable it:

1. Go to **Settings** on your TV
2. Navigate to **Apps → See All Apps** (or **Manage Installed Apps**)
3. Find **Android TV Remote Service**
4. Ensure it is **Enabled** (not disabled or restricted)

This service is the backbone of the Wi-Fi remote functionality — without it, no app can discover or connect to your TV.

### Manual IP Connection

If auto-discovery still fails, the app supports manual IP entry. Find your TV's IP address in **Settings → Network → Status** and enter it directly in the app. This bypasses discovery and connects directly.

